      "Practical wisdom for building gospel friendships. First, share meals. The table is the most ancient and most underused tool of evangelism. Inviting someone to your home for dinner says, by its very act, 'you are welcome in my life.' Second, ask real questions about their actual life and listen for their actual answers. Most people are starved for someone to take genuine interest in them. Third, be a friend across years, not seasons. The deepest evangelism happens in friendships that have weathered changes, hard times, and silences.",
      "Two warnings. First, do not bait-and-switch. If the only reason you invited a neighbour to dinner was to deliver the gospel and you were not actually interested in them as people, they will know it and you will damage the witness for years. Second, do not avoid the gospel out of false friendship. There is a kind of niceness that never tells the friend the truth — and it is not love. The believer's friendship is real, costly, and honest. The gospel comes up because we love and because it is real, not because we are running a campaign.",
