      "The disciple-maker's job is not finished when the new believer says yes to Jesus. It is not finished when the believer is baptized. It is not finished when the first thirty days are walked. It is finished — humanly speaking — when the new believer is rooted into a local church, contributing as a member, accountable to elders, and walking with brothers and sisters in real love. Anything short of this is incomplete disciple-making.",
      "Why does this matter so much? Because the New Testament knows of no Christian apart from a church. The metaphors are all corporate — Body, Bride, family, building, flock, kingdom. Paul writes letters not to individuals but to churches. Acts records not converts but congregations. The Spirit's gifts are for the building up of the Body (1 Cor 12). Solo Christianity is a modern invention, not a New Testament reality.",
